{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KLKXjutByVGgYbEWatPp3U","type":"tool_result","content":"total 536\ndrwx------ 6 agent agent   4096 Oct 27 17:39 .\ndrwx------ 9 agent agent   4096 Oct 27 17:35 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    695 Oct 27 17:35 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Oct 27 17:35 .claude\nprw------- 1 agent agent      0 Oct 27 17:39 .paper_stream_20261027_173512.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Oct 27 17:35 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3685 Oct 27 17:39 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent   4096 Oct 27 17:35 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Oct 27 17:35 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   1133 Oct 27 17:35 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    597 Oct 27 17:35 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   1069 Oct 27 17:39 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  26973 Oct 26 14:39 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   8934 Oct 26 14:39 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Oct 27 17:39 paper_agent_claude_20261027_173512.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  16496 Oct 27 17:35 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Oct 27 17:36 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Oct 27 17:35 paper_diagnostics_20261027_173512_4043.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2514 Oct 27 17:39 paper_events_20261027_173512.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    739 Oct 27 17:35 paper_prep_20261027_173512.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  43253 Oct 27 17:39 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  13903 Oct 27 17:39 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   4963 Oct 27 17:39 paper_strategy.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 174988 Oct 27 17:39 paper_strategy_agent_claude_20261027_173512.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  20784 Oct 27 17:37 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    331 Oct 27 17:39 paper_strategy_resolution.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   5389 Oct 27 17:35 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   4448 Oct 27 17:35 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  92724 Oct 27 17:36 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261027_173512.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   8445 Oct 27 17:35 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    252 Oct 27 17:36 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Oct 27 17:36 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   1399 Oct 27 17:37 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    922 Oct 27 17:37 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   8970 Oct 27 17:37 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Oct 27 17:35 results -> ../investigation\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Oct 27 17:35 source_evidence\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    252 Oct 27 17:39 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    258 Oct 27 17:39 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      7 Oct 27 17:35 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, double-blind)\n\n**Status:** ready (narrow, measurement-style paper; no improvement claim).\n\n## Title (provisional)\nHow Far Can Five Cheap Graph Statistics Go? Log-Linear Cover-Time Prediction on Small Connected Graphs\n\n## Central question\nFor connected graphs with 6<=n<=30, does a ridge model on cheap statistics (n, m, mean degree, max degree, diameter) predict Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with lower family-macro log error than the generic 2m(n-1) bound?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the sealed held-out graphs, the five-statistic log-linear ridge predictor (family-macro log MAE 0.181) is far closer to Monte Carlo cover times than the 2m(n-1) bound (1.754), a constant control (0.870) and a label-shuffled control (1.423); median Monte Carlo relative SE is about 0.029. Scope is 12 synthetic families, 24 test graphs. Which families the predictor fails on is *not* answered by the retained evidence.\n\n## Why this framing\n- The frozen selection is the seed baseline (`study.selection_kind=baseline`, investigation `completed_no_winner`, 1 attempt, 0 scored, 1 unscored, search delta 0.0). The studied method *is* the baseline, so no \"improvement over baseline\" is claimed. The comparison that exists is against the 2m(n-1) bound and controls, all in the same sealed final run.\n- Final outcome is `qualified` / `completed` / correct=true (`final.qualification.*`). Candidate diagnostics trust is `unverified`; disclose. No failed gates exist to discuss.\n- The unscored resistance/Matthews variant has no performance evidence. It appears at most as untested future work.\n- The second half of the task's question (error vs graph type) cannot be answered quantitatively: no per-family metrics in `paper_data.json`. Treat as an explicit unknown/limitation, not a failure finding.\n- Search score (-0.1913) and sealed final (-0.1814) are different scopes; never present them as an improvement.\n\n## Edit mode\nNot edit mode (placeholders unfilled, no previous_paper). `prior_paper_story_status` is nominal (\"preserved\"); nothing staged.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Five-statistic ridge predictor (proposed/studied): standardized log n, log m, log mean degree, log max degree, log diameter; ridge lambda=1, unpenalised intercept; bound fallback with no training data.\n2. 2m(n-1) bound (baseline).\n3. Constant control (baseline).\n4. Label-shuffled control (ablation-style negative control).\n5. Relabeling-invariance check (perm_max_rel_dev 0.0; supporting validity check).\n\n## Results focus\n- One direct comparison of family-macro log MAE (lower better); improvement_vs_bound 1.572 = 1.754-0.181.\n- MC uncertainty only via median relative SE 0.0287 (noise-floor context, small vs 0.181).\n- Episodes 201-203 all gave 0.18137: deterministic candidate on fixed graphs/labels; zero spread is NOT sampling uncertainty over graphs. State that graph-draw uncertainty was not measured.\n- Derived ratios (about 1/5 of constant error, about 1/10 of bound error) allowed if labelled arithmetic.\n- Unknowns: per-family, per-size errors.\n\n## Figures\n| id | type | generator | span | width | rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| overview_pipeline | diagram | generate-concept-figure | full width (single-column template, ~5.5in) | 1.0 | graph -> statistics -> ridge -> compare with MC label and bound; aggregation hierarchy; place on page 1-2 |\n| main_comparison | bar, 1 panel, 4 bars | matplotlib | full width | 0.7 | readable labels without shrinking; export PDF + 300 DPI PNG, reference PDF |\n\nNo trajectory figures. No error bars unless grounded.\n\n## Using the 8-9 page body without padding\nEvidence is thin, so space goes to: precise problem framing and why a worst-case bound is loose on small graphs; reimplementable method spec; metric/aggregation design (clipped log error, family-macro mean); a threats-to-validity section (small test set, family composition, deterministic episodes, MC labels, size range, unverified diagnostics); related work (Aleliunas et al., Feige, Lovász; only cite what is in references.bib). Do not invent experiments. If the evidence cannot honestly fill the budget, prefer substantive discussion; flag risk.\n\n## Supplementary plan\n1. Predictor specification and hyperparameters (frozen program source).\n2. Evaluation protocol and full final-metric table (`final.metric.*`).\n3. Episode records and qualification status (`final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.*`).\n4. Search-stage context, clearly separated (`study.*`).\n5. LLM usage statement (ICLR requirement).\n\n## Forbidden headings / phrases\nHeadings: Search Campaign, Iteration History, Milestones, Progression of Attempts, First Valid Result, Experiment A/B, Run Log, What We Tried.\nPhrases: correctness rate, correct and eligible, eligible, combined_score, rubric, worker_01, \"outperforms the baseline\", \"improves over the baseline\", \"state of the art\", \"generalizes to all graphs\", unsupported \"fails on\"/\"reliable on\" family claims.\nfigures:\n\nsource_evidence:\ndownstream","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"769ee4d4-a5db-48bc-9ec1-7844d9ed4d02","uuid":"98862aa5-9922-4548-827e-849d258812b3","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:39:41.341Z","tool_use_result":{"s