{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTABuMj5ZgDVKNugn7Zb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll expand the manuscript with the closed-form analysis, a metric-reading subsection, the noise floor, and a claim-status table."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1371,"cache_read_input_tokens":130846,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1371,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":67,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"769ee4d4-a5db-48bc-9ec1-7844d9ed4d02","uuid":"0558881a-3bdb-44e4-a52a-d3f1273d7293","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:45:50.449Z","request_id":"req_011CgTABt3LoHDTpxWgWFbVb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTABuMj5ZgDVKNugn7Zb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KyjTCZoMMJ2eRVxx2FAPnd","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# 1. rationale expansion + figure\nold_start=s.index(r\"\\subsection{Design rationale}\")\nold_end=s.index(r\"\\subsection{Compared predictors}\")\nnew_rat=r\"\"\"\\subsection{Design rationale}\n\\label{sec:rationale}\nWhy should a few statistics help? The ratio between $2m(n-1)$ and the true cover time depends strongly on structure, and exactly solvable families make this concrete. Writing $H_k$ for the $k$-th harmonic number and $N=n-1$, the start-averaged cover times are: $C=N^2+N(N-1)/6$ on the path (with $m=N$); $C=n(n-1)/2$ on the cycle ($m=n$); $C=(n-1)H_{n-1}$ on the complete graph (a coupon-collector argument, $m=n(n-1)/2$); and $C=2NH_{N-1}+1/(N+1)$ on the star ($m=N$). We checked these closed forms against exact subset-dynamic-programming enumeration at $n=7$ (\\texttt{scripts/verify\\_closed\\_forms.py}). % @evidence[[scripts/verify_closed_forms.py]]\nFigure~\\ref{fig:gap} plots the resulting log overshoot $\\ln[2m(n-1)/C]$. On the path it is essentially constant, about $0.54$ at $n=30$; on the cycle it is exactly $\\ln 4\\approx1.39$ at every $n$; on the star it grows like $\\ln(n/\\ln n)$ to about $2.0$ at $n=30$; and on the complete graph it grows like $\\ln(n^2/\\ln n)$ to about $5.4$ at $n=30$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#log_overshoot]]\nA single multiplicative constant therefore cannot repair the bound across families, because the required correction ranges over several nats within the studied size range. Exponents on $n$, $m$ and the diameter can in principle encode the structure that separates these regimes: dense graphs have large $m$ relative to $n$ and small diameter, path-like graphs have the opposite. The model should work to the extent that cover time on this graph population is approximately a power law in the statistics. Conversely, structurally different graphs can share all five statistics, which places a ceiling on what any function of these five numbers can achieve; we do not attempt to measure that ceiling. The closed-form curves cover only four of the twelve evaluated families and are not a prediction of the measured error of the bound on the test graphs.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\linewidth]{figures/closed_form_gap.pdf}\n\\caption{Exact log overshoot $\\ln[2m(n-1)/C]$ of the generic bound on four families with closed-form start-averaged cover times, for $6\\le n\\le 30$. The correction needed to turn the bound into a point prediction depends on the family and, for dense or star-like graphs, on $n$. These are analytic curves, not measurements.}\n\\label{fig:gap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:old_start]+new_rat+s[old_end:]\n\n# 2. reading the model and metric subsection before Experimental protocol\nrep(r\"\"\"\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n\\label{sec:protocol}\"\"\",r\"\"\"\\subsection{Reading the model and the metric}\n\\label{sec:reading}\n\\paragraph{Effective parameterisation.} Because $\\ln m=\\ln n+\\ln\\bar d-\\ln 2$, the five-feature power law is equivalent to a four-parameter family in $(\\ln n,\\ln \\bar d,\\ln\\Delta,\\ln D)$; the ridge solution distributes the shared signal over the three collinear features in the minimum-norm way. Individual fitted weights on $n$, $m$ and $\\bar d$ are therefore not separately interpretable, and we do not interpret them. Only the fitted function (the prediction) is meaningful.\n\n\\paragraph{Why log error.} Cover times in this size range span orders of magnitude, and the relevant question for a practitioner is a multiplicative one: is the prediction within a factor of $e^{\\epsilon}$ of the truth? The absolute log ratio treats over- and under-prediction symmetrically, is invariant to the units of the label, and is directly comparable between the predictor and the bound, whose predictions are on the label scale. Clipping at $10$ (a factor of $e^{10}\\approx2.2\\times10^{4}$) only limits the influence of catastrophic outputs.\n\n\\paragraph{Why family-macro aggregation.} The score averages graphs within a family first and then averages the $12$ family means with equal weight. This prevents a family with more or larger graphs from dominating the score and puts the structurally extreme families (path, lollipop, barbell) on the same footing as benign ones. The price is variance: with two test graphs per family, each family mean is a mean of two terms, and the score is sensitive to individual graphs.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n\\label{sec:protocol}\"\"\")\n\n# 3. noise floor paragraph\nrep(r\"\"\"Two cautions apply. First, this is a median:\"\"\",r\"\"\"A simple calibration helps: if the predictor were exact and each label were perturbed by a Gaussian relative error with standard deviation equal to this median, the expected absolute log error would be $\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\times0.029\\approx0.023$, about $13\\%$ of the observed $0.181$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#noise_floor_mae_gaussian]]\nThis is an order-of-magnitude statement under a Gaussian approximation, not a measured noise floor, but it indicates that label noise explains only a minor part of the ridge error. Two cautions apply. First, this is a median:\"\"\")\n\n# 4. worst-case concentration in the family subsection\nrep(r\"\"\"Which of these holds remains unknown.\"\"\",r\"\"\"Which of these holds remains unknown. Arithmetic does give a loose bound: since errors are non-negative, no family mean can exceed $12\\times0.181\\approx2.18$ (a multiplicative factor of about $8.8$), and, with two test graphs per family, no single graph's log error can exceed $24\\times0.181\\approx4.35$ (a factor of about $78$). % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#worst_case_family_mean_log_error]]\nThese worst-case concentrations are far from informative about where the error actually lies, but they show that the macro value alone already excludes a predictor that is catastrophically wrong on many families.\"\"\")\n\n# 5. claim-status table in discussion, before Conclusion\nrep(r\"\"\"\\section{Conclusion}\"\"\",r\"\"\"\\paragraph{Summary of what is and is not established.} Table~\\ref{tab:status} separates the statements that the evidence supports from those it does not.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Status of the statements a reader might want to draw from this study.}\n\\label{tab:status}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{p{0.52\\linewidth}p{0.38\\linewidth}}\n\\toprule\nStatement & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nRidge predictor has lower family-macro log MAE than the $2m(n-1)$ bound on the sealed graphs & Supported ($0.181$ vs.\\ $1.754$) \\\\\nGain comes from the five-statistic term rather than the intercept or the metric & Supported by constant and shuffled controls, as a whole \\\\\nGap is not an artefact of label noise & Supported (median relative SE $0.029$) \\\\\nPredictions are relabeling invariant & Supported (deviation $0.0$) \\\\\nWhich individual statistic drives the gain & Not tested \\\\\nWhich graph families are predicted well or badly & Unknown (not retained) \\\\\nConfidence interval over graph draws & Not measured \\\\\nBehaviour for $n>30$ or other families & Not tested \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\"\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\n(pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/dev/null; bibtex paper >/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/dev/null; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex | grep -i \"warning\\|error\\|Output written\"); $TMPDIR/venv/bin/python - <<'E'\nfrom pypdf import PdfReader\nr=PdfReader('paper.pdf'); print(len(r.pages))\nfor i,p in enumerate(r.pages): print(i+1,len(p.extract_text()), p.extract_text()[60:140].replace('\\n',' '))\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1371,"cache_read_input_tokens":130846,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1371,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":67,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"769ee4d4-a5db-48bc-9ec1-7844d9ed4d02","uuid":"19bdab86-a52d-48c2-9dfa-8baa9f5d40cf","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:46:09.171Z","request_id":"req_011CgTABt3LoHDTpxWgWFbVb"}
